use console.println

/// A vector of `n` noughts. Not the standard library: an ordinary Cove
/// function with a caller, between `main` and the operation that refuses, so
/// that the diagnostic below has a frame to blame that is neither the entry
/// point nor the machine.
fn noughts(n: Int) -> Vector<Int> {
  Vector.filled(n, 0)
}

/// Pins `Vector.filled`'s refusal of a negative length, word for word (#649).
///
/// A negative `n` names no length, so it is the call that is wrong rather
/// than an answer to give — an empty vector would hide the mistake — and the
/// run stops through `core.refuse`, as `fail_float_format_digits` does. One
/// case, because a refusal stops the run and takes every row after it;
/// `coll_vector_filled` holds the half that answers.
export fn main() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  println("{noughts(-1)}")?
  Ok(())
}
